The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham is the father of Isaac. Isaac is the father of Jacob. Jacob is the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah is the father of Perez and Zerah. Tamar is their mother. Perez is the father of Hezron. Hezron is the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab; Amminadab was the father of Nahshon; Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon is the father of Boaz. Rahab is the mother of Boaz. Boaz is the father of Obed. Ruth is the mother of Obed. Obed is the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of King David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
And Asa the father of Jehoshaphat, and Jehoshaphat the father of Joram, and Joram the father of Ahaz.
Azariah is the father of Jotham. Jotham is the father of Ahaz. Ahaz is the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
And he made Josiah the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. In that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
And afterward, the family line continued. Jehoiakim was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel is the father of Abiud. Abiud is the father of Eliakim. Eliakim is the father of Azor.
Azur is the father of Zadok. Zadok is the father of Ahihud. Ahihud is the father of Elihud.
Jews is the father of Eleazar. Eleazar is the father of Mattan. Mattan is the father of Jacob.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. Joseph is the husband of Mary. And Mary is the one who gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary had promised Joseph to marry him. But before they began to live together, it became clear that she would conceive a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Yusuf was a righteous man. He did not wish to bring her shame publicly. Therefore, he planned to divorce her quietly.
But while Joseph was thinking about this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. That is because he will save his people from their sins."
All of this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) And the name "Immanuel" means "God with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not love her, even after she bore a son. And Joseph called him Jesus.
Sages visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be the king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard about this, he was deeply disturbed. Everyone in Jerusalem was also very worried.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the people. He also called in the teachers of the law and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem in Judah," they replied, "this is what the prophet wrote: 'He said:'"
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He found out from them the exact time the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, bring me a report. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they continued on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, their hearts were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they bowed down to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they went back to their own country by another road. Jesus' family fled to Egypt
When the Magi had departed, Joseph saw a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Arise!" said the angel. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is seeking the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph climbed. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Then the word spoken by the Lord through the prophet came true: "I called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
When Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him, he became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and its vicinity. All boys who were under the age of two were to be killed. This was in accordance with the time when the Magi had seen the star.
In this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, were fulfilled. For he said
A voice is heard in Ramah. It is the voice of weeping and great mourning. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Jacob stood and took the child, as well as his mother Mary, and returned to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. And a dream warned him, so Joseph went to the region of Galilee instead.
And there he lived in a city called Nazareth. What the prophets had said about Jesus came true. They had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Turn away from your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
Indeed, John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke, saying: "A messenger is sent shouting in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight" (Isaiah 40:3).
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and from all of Judea, and they also went out from all the region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath of God?"
Produce fruits showing that you have repented of your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, "Abraham is our father." I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe has already fallen at the roots of the trees. All trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me will come one who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
So he is carrying a flail in his hand to clean the straw from his barn. And he will gather his wheat into the granary. But he will burn the husks with an unquenchable fire."The Mayor of Jesus"
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why have you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be as it is now. It is right for us to do this. It is the fulfillment of God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
When Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment, heaven was opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tested.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There the devil tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting from food, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came to him. And he said, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
He said, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down. For it is written, 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Matthew 4:5-7)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus, and angels came and cared for him. Jesus began to preach.
John was put in prison. And when Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, you fulfill what Jesus, the Prophet, said, for he said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
People who are now living in the darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But the light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chose the first disciples.
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they went away at once, leaving their nets behind, and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother. And they were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat, their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of God's kingdom. He healed every disease and illness the people had.
His news spread throughout Syria. People brought him all the sick who suffered from various diseases. Some of them were suffering from severe pain. While others were under the control of demons. Some of them were trembling violently. While others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee and the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem, Judea, and beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus blesses the people.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on a mountainside and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are those who are spiritually needy. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the humble. They will inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for what is right, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
You are blessed when people mock and hurt you because of me. You are also blessed when they tell all sorts of vicious lies about you because of me.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward in heaven is great. In the same way, people have insulted the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and Light
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will trample it underfoot.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Also, people don't light a lamp and then put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on a stand. Then the lamp illuminates everyone in the house.
"In this way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law"
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the law or the prophets. I have not come to do that. But I have come to give complete meaning to what is written.
What I tell you is truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not one smallest mark or one smallest letter of the law will pass away until all is accomplished.
Do not break even one of the least important commandments. And do not teach others to break them. If you do that, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice these commandments and teach them. Then you will be called an important person in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Beware of the scribes, who like to walk around in long robes and love greetings in the marketplaces and the first seats in the synagogues and the most respectful places at banquets. They devour widows' houses and for the sake of appearance say lengthy prayers. These will receive a harsher punishment.
You have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them, "You must not commit murder. (Exodus 20:13) Anyone who commits murder will be judged for it."
But this is what I tell you. Do not be angry with your brother. Everyone who is angry with his brother will be brought under judgment. Again, everyone who says to his brother "Raca!" must be brought under judgment before the Sanhedrin. But everyone who says "You fool!" will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Assume that you are presenting your gift at the altar. And remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle it quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. If you do not do that, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am telling you is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
I heard it said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't look at a woman in a wrong way. If anyone does that, he has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only a small part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is nothing but a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "If a man divorces his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries the divorced woman commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. I swear to God.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the promises you have made to the Lord your God. You must keep the vows you have made to Him."
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make any promises like that at all. Don't give them in heaven's name. This is God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the Earth. It is the foothold of God. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. It is the city of the Great King.
Neither swear by your heads, for you would not be able to make a hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes" and "no" mean "no". Anything more than this comes from the evil one. Be kind to others.
I heard that they said, 'An eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth.' (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But that is what I am telling you. Do not fight an evil person. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Offer him your other cheek as well.
Assume someone is taking you to court for your shirt. Let him take your coat as well.
Assume someone forces you to walk one mile. Go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not distance yourself from someone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
I heard that they said, "Love your neighbor. (Leviticus 19:18) Hate your enemy."
But here is what I say to you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
"Then you will be called children of the Most High. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet those who are from your tribe, what more are you doing than others? Even people who do not believe in God do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Helping people in need.
Beware not to do "good works" before others. Do not do them to be seen by others. If you do that, your father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to people in need, do not advertise it by blowing trumpets. Do not be like those who pretend to be pious only. They advertise what they do in temples and on the streets. They want to be honored by others. What I am going to tell you is a fact. They have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving should be secret. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer.
When you pray, don't be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the temples and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, and close the door and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees what is done in secret will reward you. For even your Father knows what things you have need of, before you ask Him. These things I speak to you, that in Me you may believe; that you may continue to believe, I give you these commands. I have spoken to you at length, because I am not going to see you again, until I say to you, 'It is I.' There is more to say about Me, for I have much more to tell you about My Father. But I do not say these things to you now, for the world cannot bear to hear them. The world will not be able to accept it, because it is not looking for it. But I know you, that you have not chosen me, but I have chosen you, and appointed you to go and bear fruit, and that your fruit should remain, so that whatever you ask of the Father, He may give it to you in abundance. My Father is glorified by this, that you bear much fruit, and show yourselves to be My disciples. As you have received from Me, so you must give to others. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the person who receives the help given by the Spirit is helped by that person's receiving the help given by the Spirit. For the
When you pray, do not babble on as the unbelievers do. For they think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Don't be like them. Dad knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name. "
May your kingdom come. Your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us. Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil when we are tempted by it. Deliver us from the accursed Satan.
Forgive others when they wrong you. If you do this, your Father in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive people their sins, then your father will not forgive your sins. Fasting
When you go without eating, don't look as sad as those who are only pretending to be holy. They make their faces look very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am going to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you go away without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father will know. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Lay up treasures in heaven
Do not lay up treasures for yourselves on earth, where moth and rust consume, and thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be filled with light.
But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be dark. If the light within you is darkness, it is very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry.
I tell you, do not be worried. Do not be worried about your life and what you will eat or drink. And do not be worried about your bodies and what you will wear. Is there more to life than just eating? Are there not more important things for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the air; for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And what is it about clothing that concerns you? Look at how the wildflowers grow. They neither toil nor spin.
But that is what I am telling you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these.
If God clothes the wild grass, would he not teach you better? After all, the grass is only here today. Tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!"
So don't worry. Don't say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People after all those things that are not of faith. Your father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do what God wants you to do. Then all those other things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. Every day has enough of its own problems.
Be just when you judge others.
Do not judge others. For you are not being judged.
You will be judged by the same standard by which you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure that you measure others.
You notice a little sawdust in your friend's eye. But you don't care about the wooden beam in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, "Let me get that little splinter out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
"O claimant! First, take the beam out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to take the speck of dust out of your friend's eye."
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before the swine. If you do that, they may trample them under their feet. Then they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask, and it will be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened for you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Whoever seeks will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
Suppose your son asked for bread. Which one of you would give him a stone?
Or if he asks for a fish, will any of you give him a snake?
Although you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the Torah and in the Prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to destruction and hell. Many people are walking down this road.
But the door is small and the way is narrow, leading to life. Only a very few people find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can tell what they really are by what they do. "Do people pick grapes from bushes or climb fig trees?"
Also, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit. And a bad tree cannot bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the person who does the will of my Father in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not drive out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then He clearly told them, "I never knew you. Depart from Me, you who practice lawlessness!" The builders, the wise and the foolish.
He who hears my words and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain comes. The water rises. The wind blows and beats against that house. But it does not fall. For it is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The rain comes. The water rises. The wind blows and beats against that house. And it falls with a high force."
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teachings.
He taught like one who had authority. He did not teach like their teachers in the law.
Jesus heals a man who was afflicted with a skin disease.
Jesus came from the mountainside. Large crowds followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. He said, "Lord, if you are willing to make me 'clean', you can do it."
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "Be clean!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman centurion believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "Oh Lord, Your servant is in his house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Lord, I am not worthy for you to come under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I tell this one, "Go," and he goes. I tell that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was astonished. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. I have not found anyone in Israel who has such faith.
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be seated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think they belong to the kingdom, they will be thrown outside, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth."
Then Jesus said to the Roman general, "Go! It will be just as you believed it would happen." And he healed his servant at that very hour. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. And she was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, many people who were possessed by demons were brought to Jesus. He cast out the spirits with a word. He healed all who were sick.
He did this to confirm the words of the prophet Isaiah: "He has carried our illnesses and suffered for our transgressions. He was wounded for our transgressions, and by his wounds, we are healed." (Isaiah 53: 4-5) Following Jesus has a cost.
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "Foxes have dens and birds of the sky have nests. But the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another follower said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a fierce storm came upon the lake. The waves beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples went and woke him up, saying, "Lord, save us! We are perishing!"
He answered, "Your faith is too small! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and commanded the winds and the waves to stop. And immediately the storm became perfectly calm.
The disciples were astonished. They asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus Heals Two Men Possessed by Demons
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. He encountered two men who were controlled by demons. They had come from the tombs. The men were so dangerous that no one could pass by that place.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come here to discipline us before our appointed time to be judged?"
It was not very far, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus saying, "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hill. They entered the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went into the city and reported all this. They told the people what had happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
Then the entire city went out to welcome Jesus. When they saw him, they pleaded with him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and arrived at his own town.
Some men brought him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Jesus saw that they had faith. He said to the man, "Do not be afraid, Son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the teachers of the law said to themselves, "This fellow is saying terrible things!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said, "Why are you thinking such evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," He said. "Take your mat and go to your home."
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And as Jesus was going on from there, he saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And rising up, Matthew followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and sinners had come and were eating with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "Those who are well do not need a doctor, but those who are sick do."
Go and learn what this means: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) I did not come for those who think they are right with God to come after me. I came for sinners to come after me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day the disciples of John came to him and said, "We and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast."
People do not put a new piece of cloth on old clothes. The new piece will separate from the old one. And this will increase the tear.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst. The wine will spill out and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. Both are preserved." Dead girl and suffering woman.
And while Jesus was speaking, a ruler came. And he knelt down before Jesus. And he said, "My daughter has just died. But come and lay your hand on her. And she will live again."
Jesus rose up and went with him. And so did his disciples.
Suddenly, a woman came from behind Jesus. She had been suffering from a disease that made her bleed. The disease had lasted for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
"I only need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed," I thought.
Jesus turned and looked at her. "Woman, do not lose heart," he said. "Your faith has healed you." The woman was healed at that very moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
And after the multitude was put out, Jesus went in and took her by the hand, and the maid arose.
The news of what Jesus had done spread throughout the region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And while Jesus was going away from there, two blind men followed him. And they cried out, saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the two blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes and said, "It will happen to you as you have believed."
They regained their sight. Jesus warned them severely, saying, "Be careful that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over that region.
While they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowds were astonished. They said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the power of the prince of demons." There are only a few laborers.
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. And He taught in their synagogues. And He preached the Gospel of the Kingdom. And He healed every sickness and every disease.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and listless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful. But there are few workers."
Ask the Lord of the Harvest to send out workers into his harvest field."
Jesus sends out the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons, and to heal all manner of disease and sickness.
These are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
Then Philip and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two were Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with these instructions: "Don't go to Gentile territories," he told them. "And don't enter any Samaritan town."
But go to the Children of Israel, for they are like stray sheep.
As you go, preach this message, "The Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Make those who have skin diseases 'clean' again. Drive out demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not take gold or silver or bronze in your belt.
Do not take a suitcase for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a city or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at this person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If that house receives you, bless it with peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I tell you is true. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be brought before the governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for the Gentiles.
But when you are arrested, do not worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the appropriate words that should be said will be given to you.
You will not be the speaker. The spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will betray each other to be killed. Fathers will betray their sons. Sons will revolt against their fathers and execute them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I will tell you is the truth. You will not complete the circuit of the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
No student surpasses his teacher. And no servant surpasses his master.
It is sufficient for a student to be like his master. And it is sufficient for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house has been named Beelzebub, what may be expected of the others who live there?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed. What is hidden will be brought to light.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the spirit. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Is it not the case that selling two birds for a farthing? But not one of them falls to the ground without your father knowing about it.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Do not be afraid, you are more valuable than many sparrows.
And what about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to reverse "sons over their fathers. Daughters will reject obeying their mothers. Aunts will be against their mothers-in-law."
The man's enemies will be his own family members. (Micah 7:6)
Everyone who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Everyone who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
Whoever does not bear their own cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. And if anyone loses his life because of me, he will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me, and whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Assume someone greets a prophet as a prophet. That person will receive the reward of a prophet. And assume someone greets a saint as a saint. That person will receive the reward of a saint.
Assume that someone even gave a cup of cold water to a little child following me. What I am going to tell you is a fact. That person will definitely be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples, and then he continued his teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. And when he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is coming, or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see."
The blind regain their sight. The disabled are able to walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised. The good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me."
And while the disciples of Jacob and John were coming out, Jesus began to speak regarding Jacob in front of the crowd, saying: "What did you come out to see in the wilderness? A reed shaken by the wind?"
And if not, what did you go out to see? A finely dressed man? No. The people who are finely dressed are in the palaces of kings.
What then did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one mentioned in the Holy Book. The verse says: "I will send my messenger to you before my arrival. He will prepare the way for you." (Malachi 3: 1)
What I am saying to you is true. No one was born more important than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than John the Baptist.
For John the Baptist's days, the Kingdom of Heaven has been advancing forcefully. And powerful people are the ones who take control over it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept it, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let those who have ears hear.
What can I match people with today? They are like children sitting in the markets and inviting others. They say,
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not become sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is justified by her deeds." Woe to those cities that are departed from the fear of the Lord, from the love of the Lord, and from the obedience to the commandments of the Lord.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. The people there had not left their sins behind. And He said:
"Woe to you, Korazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes."
But I tell you, it will be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon in the day of judgment than for you.
And what about you, Capernaum? Will you be lifted up to heaven? No! You will descend to the place of the dead. Suppose the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom. It would still be here today.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who feel tired
Jesus said at that time, "I praise you, Father. You are the Lord of heaven and earth. You have hidden these things from the wise and learned. But you have shown them to little children."
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
"My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to make known to him."
Come to me, all of you who are weary and carry heavy burdens, and I will give you rest.
Become my slaves and learn from me. I am merciful and righteous in might. You will find rest for your souls.
Serving me is easy, and my burden is light."
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick the heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to Jesus, "Look! This man is a Sabbath breaker. Yet your disciples are doing it too!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did? He and his companions were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his companions ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Have you read the Shariah? It tells us how the priests in the temple should perform their work every Sabbath day, but they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that the one who is greater than the temple is here.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6: 6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not bring charges against those who are not guilty.
"The Son of man is Lord of the Sabbath."
From there, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a well on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you lift it out?"
A man is worth more than sheep! So the laws allow us to do good on the Sabbath."
Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and it was restored, good as new, just like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. Abdullah The Chosen One
Jesus knew all the plans of the Pharisees. So he left that place. And many followed him, and he healed them of every disease.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
This would have fulfilled what was said through the Prophet Jesus. It says:
This is my servant, whom I have chosen, him do ye trust: for he shall bring forth the true words, he shall not be frustrated nor shall he be put to shame: and none of their enemies shall escape.
He will not quarrel or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bending branch. He will not extinguish a flickering dim flame. He will fix everything.
He will not fail nor be discouraged until he establishes justice on the Earth. He will not fail nor be discouraged until he establishes justice on the Earth.
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man could speak and see things.
All the people were amazed. "Can this be the Son of David?" they said.
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. "Every kingdom divided against itself will be ruined," Jesus told them. "And every city or household divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By what power, then, do your people cast them out? They will be your judges.
But suppose I am casting out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and take what the man owns? You must first tie him up. Then you can plunder his house.
Whoever is not with me is against me. Whoever does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
Here is what I say. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. Such a person will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you make a good tree, its fruit will be good. And if you make a bad tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree from its fruit.
Your nest is made of poisonous snakes! How can you evil people say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts.
A good man says good things. These come from the goodness that is placed within him. An evil man says evil things. These come from the evil that is placed within him.
But that's what I'm telling you. On the day of reckoning, people will have to give an account for every careless word they have spoken.
In your own words, you will be condemned or you will not be condemned." The miraculous sign of Jonah.
They said, "Teacher, we want you to show us a remarkable sign."
He answered, "Evil and unbelieving people seek a miraculous sign! But no one will be given a sign except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.
The men of Nineveh will stand up on the day of judgment with the people who are living now. And the men of Nineveh will prove that these people are guilty. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now, someone is here who is more important than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise on the day of resurrection with the living. And she will prove that they are guilty. She came from afar to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now, there is one who is more important than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a man? It goes through the dry areas, looking for a place to rest. But it doesn't find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. She had managed to clean the house and put it in good condition.
Then the unclean spirit goes out and takes with itself seven other spirits more wicked than itself. They go in and live there. That man is in a worse state than before. This is how it will be with the wicked of today." The mother and brothers of Jesus
And Jesus was still speaking to the crowds, when his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to speak to him.
He was told: "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples and said, "Behold, my mother! Behold, my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that day, Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd of people gathered around him. So he got into a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using parables. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seed.
He scattered the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Another seed fell among the thorns. The thorn grew and choked the plant.
And others said: "The seed that fell on the good earth has become a rope a hundred times, sixty times, and thirty times what was sown."
Let those who have ears hear.
The students came to him. They asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "I have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. That has not been given to foreigners."
To whoever has this kind of knowledge, more will be given. Indeed, they will have much. From whoever does not have this kind of knowledge, even the little that they have will be taken.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they do not really see. They listen, but they do not really hear or understand."
In them, the words of the prophet Jesus are fulfilled. He said, "You will hear but not understand. You will see but not recognize what you are seeing."
The hearts of these people have become callous; they barely hear with their ears, they have closed their eyes, otherwise they might see with their eyes, hear with their ears, understand with their hearts, and repent to the Lord, and then he would heal them. (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
Blessed are your eyes because they see, and blessed are your ears because they hear.
What I am telling you is the truth. Many prophets and holy people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here's the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seeds that fell in rocky places. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So they do not last long. They soon abandon their faith when trouble or suffering comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are those who hear the message. But then, the anxieties of life and the false promises of wealth choke them, and they fail to bear fruit.
Some others, however, received the seeds that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60, or 30 times more than what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. "Thus is the kingdom of heaven," he said. "A man sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's slaves came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did the weeds come from?"
He said, "One of the enemies did this." The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "When you pull out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Let them grow together until harvest. At that time I will tell the reapers what to do. Here is what I will say to them. First, gather the weeds. Tie them into bundles to be burned. Then, gather the wheat. Bring it to my barn." "The parables of the mustard seed and the yeast"
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that one of you took and sowed in his field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest of all garden plants. And it becomes a tree. Birds come and rest in its branches."
Jesus told them another story. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman mixed yeast into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
Jesus spoke to the crowd about all these things using parables. He did not tell them anything without telling a parable.
Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weed in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seeds is the Son of Man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the workers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled out and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send out his angels. They will remove from his kingdom all causes of sin. They will also throw out the people who do evil.
They will cast them into the furnace of fire. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Let him who has ears hear. Stories of the hidden treasure and pearls.
"The kingdom of heaven is like treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he went and sold all that he had and bought that field."
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of rare pearls.
And he found one of great value. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The Story of the Net
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake, and gathered in it all kind of fishes.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish into the sea.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The wicked will be cast into the fiery furnace. There will the wicked wail and gnash their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them, "To what can I compare the children of man who are like the ones who sit in the marketplace to whom the people bring their cases, and these, when they hear them, pronounce judgment. To what can I compare this generation? It is like children sitting in the marketplace calling out to one another, 'We played the flute for you, but you did not dance; we sang a lament, but you did not weep.' For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, 'He has a demon'; the Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say, 'Behold, a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!' But wisdom is justified by her deeds." And he said, "What is the kingdom of God like? To what can I compare it? It is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in his field. It grew and became a tree, and the birds of the air came and nested in its branches." And he said to them, "To what can I compare this generation? It is like children sitting in the marketplace calling out to one another, 'We played the flute for you, but you did not dance; we sang a lament, but you did not weep.' For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, 'He has a demon'; the Son of Man came eating and drinking, and they say, 'Behold, a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!' But wisdom is justified by her deeds." And he said to the disciples, "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few; therefore pray earnestly to the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest." And he went throughout Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom, and his fame spread throughout all Syria, and they brought to him all who were ill with various diseases and pains, and demoniacs and epileptics and paralytics, and he healed them. And large crowds followed him from Galilee and Decapolis and from Jerusalem and Judea and from beyond the Jordan. And when a multitude of people had come together, who had not brought any food, he said to his disciples, "I have compassion on the crowd, because they have been with me now for three days, and they have nothing to eat; for if I send them away hungry to their homes, they will faint on the way; and some of them came from a great distance." And his disciples answered him, "Where could we find enough bread in this desolate place to satisfy such a large crowd?" And Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" And they said, "Seven, and a few small fish." And he directed the crowd to sit down on the ground; and he took the seven loaves and the few fish, and looking up to heaven, he said a blessing and broke the loaves and gave them to his disciples to set before the crowd; and he divided the fish among them all. And everyone ate and was satisfied; and they took up what remained, seven large baskets full; and they were about five thousand men who had eaten. And immediately Jesus made his disciples get into a boat and go before him to the other side, while he sent the crowd off. And after he had sent the crowd off, he went up on the mountain by himself to pray; and when evening came, he was there alone. And by this time the crowd was large and had nothing to eat; and many of them would have gone home, had they not heard that he was coming. And when Jesus came, he found that they had no bread, and he was concerned for them. And he said to his disciples, "I am concerned for this crowd, for they have been with me for three days and have nothing to eat; and if I send them away hungry to their homes, they will faint on the way; and some of them came from a great distance." And his disciples answered him, "Where could we find enough bread in this desolate place to satisfy such a large crowd?" And Jesus said to them, "How many loaves do you have?" And they said, "Seven, and a few
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown Nazareth. And there he began to teach people in their synagogue. And they were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country, and in his own house."
Because they did not believe, few miracles were performed there.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod Antipas arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod Philip's brother.
John said to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wants to kill John, but he is afraid of the people, because they think that John is a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias' daughter danced before Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He promised her on oath that he would give her anything she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large plate."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and his guests for dinner. So he told one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod had John beheaded in prison.
And his head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowds heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep compassion for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples went to him and said, "This is a deserted place, and it is already late. Send the crowds away, so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus answered: "It is not necessary for them to go. You give them something to eat."
They answered, "We have five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus directed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples gathered twelve baskets full of leftovers.
There were about 5000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. And he commanded them to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowd away.
And after he sent them, he went up to a mountain by himself to pray. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The ship was already far from land. It was being tossed about by the waves because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake, so they were afraid. "He is a ghost!" they said. And they cried from fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, are you You?" Peter asked. "If You are, tell me to come to You on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. "Your faith is too small!" He said. "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind stopped.
Then those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message into the surrounding countryside. And people brought all their sick to Jesus for healing.
They asked him to allow the sick to touch only the hem of his garment. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They asked:
Why do your disciples not live according to the teachings of the elders? Your disciples don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) and He also said, "If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help that you have received from us is a gift intended for God."
They do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
You are hidden ones! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
They honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus came to the crowds and called out to them, saying, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make it 'unclean'. It is what comes out of your mouth that makes it 'unclean'."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So it is with murder, adultery, and sexual immorality in general. Also, theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus went out from Galilee and entered the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me!" A demon had control over her daughter. She was suffering greatly.
Jesus did not say a single word. His disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Send her away. She is still screaming after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
Re: "It is not right that you take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
She said, "Yes, Lord." "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' tables."
Then Jesus answered: "Woman, great is your faith! It will be done for you as you ask." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus went out from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the side of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought him a blind man and a lame man, and they brought him those who were maimed, and they brought him the deaf, and they brought him many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. Therefore, the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have already been with me for three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will become too weak on their journey home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
"How many loaves do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowds: Sit on the ground.
The seven loaves and the fish were taken, and he thanked God. Then he broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftovers.
The number of men who ate food was 4000. Women and children also ate food.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. He went to the region near Magadan.
People ask Jesus for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
Re: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: "It will be fine. The sky is red."
And in the morning you say, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and overcast." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and unbelieving generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it but the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out, beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this matter among themselves. They said, "He must be saying this because we did not bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "Why do you talk to each other about having no bread? For you do not discern."
Do you not yet understand? Do you not remember the five loaves and the two thousand people? Do you not remember how many baskets you gathered?
Do you remember the seven loaves and the four thousand people? Do you remember how many baskets of leftovers you collected?
How is it possible that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not telling them to beware of the leaven used in bread. He was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. And Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and others Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the Living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For this was not revealed to you by anyone, but by my Father who is in heaven."
Thus I tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. And whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed and rise to life again on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. You do not have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, they must say no to themselves. They must take up their cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses it for me, he will find it.
What is the benefit of winning the whole world if a person loses themselves? Or what could anyone trade for their soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father. He will come with His angels, and He will reward each person according to what they have done.
What I am saying to you is true. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus appeared changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He led them up to a high mountain. They were all by themselves.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice came from the cloud, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces in the dust.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said. "Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. While coming down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "That is true. It is supposed to be Elijah who comes and makes everything new again."
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near to the crowd, a man approached Jesus. He knelt down before him.
He said, "Lord, have mercy on my son. He is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
"O people, you are wicked and evil!" Jesus answered. "How long must I stay with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the child to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and asked, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I am about to tell you is true. If your faith is as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there.' And it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
He will kill him. On the third day, he will rise from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, it is so," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Whose sons here on earth are kings to whom taxes and tribute are paid? Do they collect these from their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter replied. "So that the children will not have to pay," Jesus told him.
But we do not want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line out. Take the first fish that you catch. Open its mouth. In there you will find the exact coin that you need. Take it and give it to them for my tax and my.
